The [original incident](/kb/incidents/4471/) calls this one known issue with one queued fix.
Its rendered cause table contradicts that conclusion: the shared parser and the booking form hint are separate problems.
Each cause has its own ticket. This review combines the report export through 4 October 2026
with the later engineering update on the course page. The dispositions below are reported by the desk;
they are not independent verification of the production service.

<Callout kind="warn">
Read the generated evidence before relying on the page's prose. “Known” is a desk status, not proof that all causes have tickets or fixes.
</Callout>

## Count by report ID, classify by tenant and entry path

<IncidentReview />

The calculation follows `uniqueById` and `groupByCause` in `src/lib/kb/causes.ts`, joined to the tenant directory through `locales.ts`.
`Intl.DateTimeFormat(...).formatToParts(...)` determines day-first locale order. Neither a browser locale nor `meta.intakeTag` identifies the tenant's configured date convention.

## Two failure paths, two tickets

**Shared parser.** For day-first tenants, the shared parser assumes month first.
This covers app and email reports, plus form reports through booking-v2. For example, `R-41200`
describes 7 April becoming 4 July. `R-41205` belongs here because Morrow Tax is configured as `fr-FR`,
even though its browser reports `en-US` and intake guessed `hint`.
The engineering update reports this path fixed by `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
The ticket retains the acceptance criteria for checking ambiguous day/month inputs, invalid dates, and month-first controls.

**Wrong booking-v3 hint.** For a day-first tenant using booking-v3, the form prints `MM/DD/YYYY`
while interpreting the typed value using the tenant's convention. Only the form channel takes this path.
For example, `R-41230` comes through Copper Studio's booking-v3 form (`es-ES`), although intake calls it `parser`.
The desk reports this path **won't fix** because the booking-v3 form was retired.
Retirement removes this reported entry path; it does not mean the hint was corrected.
Changing the shared parser alone would not have corrected misleading form guidance.

These mechanisms come from the supplied executable model and tenant configuration. The export contains no production parser or form implementation to patch or independently reproduce.

## Read the queue in the right order

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The weekly plot uses `receivedAt` for arrivals and `mergedAt` for desk merges. Reports accumulated before the large July merge.
An administrative burst must not be described as an incident onset. The export continues receiving reports after the bulk merge;
the original page's confident “one fix queued” wording hid a second workstream that later received a different disposition.

## Triage by cause; keep unknown cases apart

The [old triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe/) instructs the desk to merge matching subjects, but its own explorer does not implement that rule.
Its review date is stale under the shared layout's review policy. A familiar title and an accepted status do not make that advice current.

1. Read the report and resolve its tenant, configured locale, form version, and channel.
2. Compare the failure mechanism with an existing ticket. A matching cause can have different wording or arrive through another channel.
3. Keep different causes apart even if their subject lines are identical. Form version and tenant configuration can change the classification.
4. If the tenant or cause is unknown, investigate before merging. Unknown does not mean duplicate.
5. Count one report per ID and preserve the intake label as a historical guess. Reconcile the split totals to the source ticket.

<DedupeExplorer />

The explorer shows both kinds of subject-matching error and refuses to merge reports with no tenant on file.
The [replacement decision proposal](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause/) records this recommendation.
Decision 0001 remains unchanged as the historical accepted record; desk acceptance of the replacement is still needed.

## Follow-up and limits

Keep the one-day descriptions visible while collecting their original input, timezone, rendered invite, and event timestamp.
Do not subtract them again from the computed parser group or present a timezone theory as a confirmed third bug.
Rebuild this page when reports or tenant settings change, and re-read its prose when either the evidence or review status changes.
The desk's closed status is supported by the later engineering update, not by treating all reports as one repaired cause.
The one-day descriptions still need evidence before their individual mechanisms can be verified.

